/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/terraform.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("terraform", [["tf1",0,1,"A team rebuilds a test environment by hand each sprint and the builds never match. Which IaC benefit most directly fixes this?",["Lower cloud list prices for the same resources","Faster network throughput between the servers","Repeatable builds from the same versioned code","Automatic replacement of the operating system"],2,"Code in version control produces the same environment every time. IaC doesn't change prices, bandwidth or OS choice.","Objective 1b",["IaC changes how resources are defined, not what the cloud provider charges for them, so prices stay the same.","Network throughput depends on instance types and network design, not on whether the environment was built by code or by hand.",null,"IaC does not automatically swap operating systems; the OS is whatever image the code specifies, and the problem here is inconsistency between builds."],null],["tf2",0,1,"Which statement best describes Terraform's declarative approach?",["You describe the end state and Terraform works out the steps","You write each API call yourself in the exact order Terraform must run it","You record console clicks and Terraform replays them","You write shell scripts that Terraform schedules"],0,"Terraform configuration declares the desired result; Terraform builds the dependency graph and decides the actions. Listing each call in order is the imperative style.","Objective 1a",[null,"Writing each API call in a fixed order is the imperative style; in Terraform you declare resources and it builds the call order from the dependency graph.","Terraform does not record or replay console clicks; it reads configuration files and calls provider APIs to reach the declared state.","Terraform does not schedule shell scripts; that describes a scripted, imperative approach rather than declaring the desired end state."],null],["tf3",0,1,"A company runs workloads on AWS and Azure and manages DNS with Cloudflare. How can Terraform help?",["It converts AWS resources into equivalent Azure resources whenever asked","It needs a separate Terraform edition for each cloud","It can only manage one provider per state file","One workflow and language manage all three through providers"],3,"Providers let a single configuration and plan/apply workflow cover many platforms. Terraform doesn't translate resources between clouds, and one state can hold several providers.","Objective 1c",["Terraform has no feature that translates AWS resources into Azure ones; each provider has its own resource types that you write separately.","There is one Terraform CLI for every platform; support for each cloud comes from provider plugins, not separate editions.","A single state file can track resources from many providers, such as AWS, Azure and Cloudflare in one configuration.",null],null],["tf4",0,1,"An engineer runs terraform apply twice in a row without changing code or infrastructure. What happens on the second run?",["Every resource is recreated from scratch","Terraform reports no changes to make","Terraform duplicates each resource","The state file is deleted and rebuilt"],1,"Terraform is idempotent: it compares configuration to current state and acts only on differences, so there is nothing to do.","Objective 1b",["Terraform only acts on differences; with no changes to code or infrastructure it has no reason to recreate anything.",null,"Terraform tracks each resource in state and knows it already exists, so a second apply does not create duplicates.","State is not deleted on apply; Terraform reads and updates it, and with no changes it stays effectively the same."],null],["tf5",0,1,"Which task is Terraform designed for, compared with a configuration management tool?",["Editing application config files inside running VMs","Provisioning and managing the lifecycle of infrastructure","Patching operating system packages on long-lived servers every night","Collecting logs from servers and sending alerts"],1,"Terraform provisions and manages infrastructure resources through APIs. Package patching and in-guest configuration are what tools like Ansible or Chef focus on.","Objective 1a",["Editing config files inside running VMs is in-guest configuration, which is the job of configuration management tools like Ansible or Chef.",null,"Nightly OS package patching on long-lived servers is configuration management work, and it also runs against Terraform's immutable-infrastructure approach.","Log collection and alerting is a monitoring or observability function, not something Terraform does (though it can provision the monitoring tools)."],null],["tf6",0,1,"A security lead wants every infrastructure change reviewed before it happens. Which IaC practice supports this?",["Giving every engineer console admin rights","Running terraform apply -auto-approve on laptops","Letting engineers make changes first and write up documentation afterwards","Storing configuration in Git and reviewing plans in pull requests"],3,"Version-controlled code lets changes and their plans be reviewed and approved. Broad console access and auto-approve bypass review.","Objective 1b",["Broad console admin rights let people change infrastructure directly, bypassing any review of the change.","-auto-approve skips the approval prompt, and running from laptops has no shared review step, so this removes review rather than adding it.","Documenting after the fact means the change already happened unreviewed; the requirement is review before the change.",null],null],["tf7",0,1,"What lets Terraform manage services such as GitHub teams or Datadog monitors, not just cloud servers?",["A provider plugin for each service's API","Terraform's built-in SaaS connector","A special Terraform edition built only for SaaS tools","Manual steps recorded in the state file"],0,"Any service with an API can have a provider, which is why Terraform is service-agnostic. There is no special SaaS edition.","Objective 1c",[null,"Terraform has no built-in SaaS connector; SaaS services are supported through provider plugins like any other platform.","There is no SaaS-only Terraform edition; the same CLI manages any service that has a provider.","State records managed objects and their attributes; it cannot perform manual steps or make Terraform able to talk to a new service."],null],["tf8",0,2,"A required_providers entry reads: aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\", version = \"~> 5.40\" }. Which version could terraform init select?",["6.0.0","5.39.0","5.72.1","4.67.0"],2,"`~> 5.40` allows 5.40 and any later 5.x release, but not 6.0. 5.39 is below the minimum.","Objective 2a",["~> 5.40 only lets the minor and patch parts increase, so it excludes the next major version 6.0.0.","5.39.0 is lower than the 5.40 minimum that ~> 5.40 sets.",null,"4.67.0 is an earlier major version and is below the 5.40 minimum."],null],["tf9",0,2,"A developer runs terraform init and gets a newer provider patch than a teammate did last week. What stops this drift between machines?",["Committing the .terraform directory","Deleting the local provider plugin cache before every run","Setting TF_LOG to TRACE","Committing the .terraform.lock.hcl file"],3,"The dependency lock file records the selected provider versions and checksums; committed to VCS, everyone gets the same builds. The .terraform directory holds downloaded plugins and should not be committed.","Objective 2a",["The .terraform directory holds downloaded plugins and modules for one machine and should be ignored by Git; it does not pin versions for others.","Clearing the plugin cache just forces a fresh download, which would still pick the newest matching version without a lock file.","TF_LOG only controls log verbosity and has no effect on which provider version is chosen.",null],null],["tf10",0,2,"The lock file pins aws 5.40.0, and the constraint ~> 5.40 would allow 5.72. How do you move to the newest allowed version?",["Run terraform validate","Run terraform apply -refresh-only","Run terraform init -upgrade","Delete terraform.tfstate"],2,"`init -upgrade` ignores the locked versions, picks the newest within the constraints and updates the lock file. Validate and refresh-only don't touch providers.","Objective 2a",["terraform validate only checks configuration syntax and consistency; it never downloads or changes provider versions.","A refresh-only apply updates state to match real infrastructure and does not touch providers or the lock file.",null,"Deleting the state file loses Terraform's record of your resources and does nothing to the lock file's provider versions."],null],["tf11",0,2,"What is a Terraform provider?",["A file that stores the current resource attributes","A plugin that lets Terraform call a platform's API","A reusable folder of resources with inputs","The company hosting your remote state"],1,"Providers are plugins that implement resource types and data sources by calling APIs. State stores attributes; modules are reusable folders.","Objective 2b",["That describes the state file, which records resource attributes; providers are the plugins that manage those resources.",null,"A reusable folder of resources with input variables is a module, not a provider.","The company or service that stores remote state is a backend; a provider is a plugin that talks to a platform's API."],null],["tf12",0,2,"A configuration has provider \"aws\" { region = \"us-east-1\" } and provider \"aws\" { alias = \"west\" region = \"us-west-2\" }. How does a resource use the second one?",["provider = aws.west","region = aws.west","provider = \"aws-west\"","alias = \"west\" on the resource"],0,"The `provider` meta-argument takes an unquoted reference `<name>.<alias>`. Resources don't take an `alias` argument.","Objective 2c",[null,"region is an ordinary provider or resource argument that expects a string; it cannot select a provider configuration.","The provider meta-argument takes an unquoted reference in the form aws.west, not a quoted string like \"aws-west\".","alias is set only in the provider block; a resource chooses a configuration with the provider meta-argument, not an alias argument."],null],["tf13",0,2,"source = \"hashicorp/random\" is shorthand for which full provider address?",["github.com/hashicorp/random","releases.hashicorp.com/providers/random","registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/random","app.terraform.io/hashicorp/random"],2,"When no hostname is given, Terraform uses the public registry at registry.terraform.io.","Objective 2a",["Provider source addresses default to the public Terraform Registry, not GitHub, even though many providers are developed there.","releases.hashicorp.com hosts binary downloads, but it is not the default hostname in a provider source address.",null,"app.terraform.io is HCP Terraform's hostname, used for private registry providers only when written out explicitly."],null],["tf14",0,2,"Which is NOT a purpose of Terraform state?",["Storing the provider plugin binaries","Mapping resources in code to real object IDs","Tracking metadata such as dependencies","Caching attributes to speed up planning"],0,"Provider binaries live in the .terraform directory or a plugin cache. State maps real objects, tracks metadata and caches attributes.","Objective 2d",[null,"Mapping configuration to real object IDs is a core purpose of state, so it cannot be the exception.","Tracking metadata such as dependencies (for correct destroy ordering) is one of state's documented purposes.","Caching resource attributes to speed up plans on large infrastructures is one of state's documented purposes."],null],["tf15",0,2,"A database password is set through a sensitive variable. Where can the value still be read in plain text?",["In the terraform plan console output","In the output of terraform fmt","Nowhere; sensitive values are encrypted","In the terraform.tfstate file"],3,"`sensitive` only redacts CLI display. The value is still stored in state, so state must be protected.","Objective 2d",["Terraform redacts sensitive values in plan output, showing (sensitive value) instead of the password.","terraform fmt only reformats configuration files and prints file names; it does not output variable values.","sensitive = true only hides values in CLI output; it does not encrypt them, and they remain readable in state.",null],null],["tf16",0,2,"A terraform block contains required_version = \">= 1.12.0\". An engineer runs Terraform 1.9. What happens?",["Terraform upgrades itself automatically","Terraform returns an error before doing any work","The setting only affects providers","Terraform continues the run but prints a version warning first"],1,"`required_version` constrains the Terraform CLI version; a version outside the range makes Terraform stop with an error.","Objective 2a",["Terraform never upgrades itself; you must install a CLI version that satisfies the constraint.",null,"required_version constrains the Terraform CLI itself; provider versions are set in required_providers.","A version outside the required_version range is a hard error, not a warning, and Terraform will not continue the run."],null],["tf17",0,2,"Which registry tier describes providers owned and maintained by HashiCorp?",["Partner","Official","Community","Archived"],1,"Official providers are owned and maintained by HashiCorp. Partner providers are maintained by technology partners, and community providers by individuals or groups.","Objective 2b",["Partner providers are written and maintained by third-party technology companies, not by HashiCorp.",null,"Community providers are published by individuals or groups in the community, not maintained by HashiCorp.","Archived marks providers that are no longer maintained; it is not the tier for providers HashiCorp actively owns."],null],["tf18",0,3,"An engineer clones a repository with Terraform code and wants to run a plan. What must run first?",["terraform apply","terraform refresh","terraform fmt","terraform init"],3,"init sets up the backend and downloads providers and modules; plan fails without it.","Objective 3b",["apply also needs initialized providers and a backend, so it cannot run before init either.","terraform refresh (deprecated) also needs an initialized directory, and it does not install providers or modules.","fmt can reformat files without init, but it does not prepare the directory, so plan would still fail.",null],null],["tf19",0,3,"Which step in the core workflow lets a reviewer see exactly what will be created, changed or destroyed?",["Plan","Write","Init","Validate"],0,"`terraform plan` compares configuration with state and real infrastructure and shows proposed actions.","Objective 3a",[null,"Write is where you author configuration; it does not compare anything against real infrastructure or show proposed actions.","init prepares the working directory by installing providers and modules; it does not calculate any changes.","validate only checks syntax and internal consistency and never shows what will be created, changed or destroyed."],null],["tf20",0,3,"A pipeline must fail if any .tf file is not in canonical format, without rewriting files. Which command fits?",["terraform validate -json -no-color","terraform plan -lock=false","terraform fmt -check -recursive","terraform init -reconfigure"],2,"`fmt -check` exits non-zero when files need formatting and changes nothing; `-recursive` includes subdirectories. validate checks correctness, not style.","Objective 3g",["validate checks that the configuration is correct, not that it is formatted; -json and -no-color only change the output format.","plan -lock=false runs a plan without state locking; it does not check formatting and is unsafe to use by default.",null,"init -reconfigure resets backend configuration and has nothing to do with file formatting."],null],["tf21",0,3,"What does terraform validate check?",["That the provider credentials are valid and have enough permissions","That resources exist in the cloud account","That the state file matches reality","That the syntax and references are internally consistent"],3,"validate checks syntax, types and references in the configuration without calling provider APIs or reading remote state.","Objective 3c",["validate does not contact provider APIs, so it cannot test credentials or permissions; plan or apply would reveal those problems.","validate does not query the cloud account, so it cannot tell whether resources exist.","Comparing state with reality is what a refresh during plan (or plan -refresh-only) does; validate does not read remote state.",null],null],["tf22",0,3,"A team wants CI to apply exactly the plan a reviewer approved. Which pair of commands does this?",["terraform plan, then a separate terraform apply -auto-approve","terraform validate, then terraform apply","terraform plan -out=tfplan, then terraform apply tfplan","terraform refresh, then terraform apply"],2,"A saved plan file freezes the reviewed actions and apply runs it without a prompt. A fresh apply could produce a different plan.","Objective 3d",["A separate apply computes a fresh plan, which may differ from the one reviewed if code or infrastructure changed in between.","validate produces no plan to review, and a plain apply creates a new plan at apply time.",null,"terraform refresh only updates state and is deprecated; the following apply still builds an unreviewed new plan."],null],["tf23",0,3,"In a plan, a resource is shown with the symbol -/+. What does it mean?",["It will be updated in place without replacement","It will be destroyed and then recreated","It will be read as a data source","It will be imported into state"],1,"-/+ means replacement: destroy then create. ~ is an in-place update.","Objective 3d",["An in-place update is shown with ~; -/+ means the object cannot be updated in place and must be replaced.",null,"Data source reads are shown with <=, not -/+.","Imports appear as an import action in the plan; -/+ shows destroy-then-create replacement."],null],["tf24",0,3,"A container is healthy but you need Terraform to recreate it. What is the current recommended command?",["terraform apply -replace=docker_container.web","terraform taint docker_container.web, then terraform apply","terraform state rm docker_container.web","terraform destroy -auto-approve"],0,"`-replace` plans a replacement for that one resource and shows it in the plan. taint is deprecated; state rm would forget the resource instead.","Objective 3e",[null,"terraform taint still works in some versions but is deprecated, and it marks the resource in state without showing the change in a reviewable plan first.","state rm makes Terraform forget the container while leaving it running; the next apply would try to create a duplicate rather than replace it.","destroy -auto-approve removes every resource in the state, not just the one container, and it does not recreate anything."],null],["tf25",0,3,"How can you preview what terraform destroy would remove without deleting anything?",["terraform destroy -check","terraform validate -destroy","terraform plan -destroy","terraform show -destroy"],2,"`plan -destroy` creates a speculative destroy plan. destroy has no -check option.","Objective 3f",["terraform destroy has no -check option; the way to preview a destroy is plan -destroy.","validate has no -destroy option and never produces a plan of any kind.",null,"terraform show displays existing state or a saved plan; it has no -destroy option to build a destroy preview."],null],["tf26",0,3,"After adding a new module block to the configuration, terraform plan reports \"Module not installed\". What fixes it?",["terraform init","terraform fmt","terraform validate","terraform output"],0,"init (or terraform get) downloads new modules. Any new provider or module requires init.","Objective 3b",[null,"fmt only reformats files; it does not download modules.","validate itself needs modules installed and reports the same missing-module problem rather than fixing it.","terraform output reads output values from state and cannot install modules."],null],["tf27",0,3,"You moved the backend to a new bucket but don't want to copy the old state, just start using the new settings. Which command applies?",["terraform init -upgrade","terraform apply -refresh-only","terraform validate -backend","terraform init -reconfigure"],3,"`-reconfigure` ignores the existing backend setup and configures the new one without migrating state. `-migrate-state` would copy it.","Objective 3b",["init -upgrade updates providers and modules to the newest allowed versions; it does not change how the backend is configured.","A refresh-only apply updates state from real infrastructure; it does not switch backends.","validate has no -backend option, and validate never configures backends.",null],null],["tf28",0,3,"Running terraform apply without a saved plan does what before making changes?",["Applies the changes immediately without showing any output","Creates a plan and asks you to approve it","Runs terraform fmt on all files","Destroys all resources first"],1,"Without a plan file, apply generates a plan and waits for you to type yes (unless -auto-approve is used).","Objective 3e",["Without a saved plan, apply always shows the plan first and asks for approval unless -auto-approve is set.",null,"apply never runs terraform fmt; formatting is a separate command.","apply only makes the changes in the plan; it does not destroy all resources first."],null],["tf29",0,3,"Which plan symbol shows an attribute being updated without replacing the resource?",["+","~","-","<="],1,"~ means update in place. + is create, - is destroy, and <= marks a data source read.","Objective 3d",["+ means a resource will be created, not updated.",null,"- means a resource will be destroyed, not updated.","<= marks a data source being read during the run, not a managed resource update."],null],["tf30",0,3,"A teammate wants to pass a different variable file for staging at plan time. Which syntax is correct?",["terraform plan -tfvars staging","terraform plan -tfvars-file=env/staging.tfvars","terraform plan -input=staging.tfvars","terraform plan -var-file=staging.tfvars"],3,"`-var-file` loads variable values from the named file. The other flags don't exist or mean something else (-input controls prompting).","Objective 3d",["There is no -tfvars option on terraform plan; variable files are passed with -var-file.","-tfvars-file is not a real option; the correct name is -var-file.","-input controls whether Terraform prompts for missing variables (true or false); it does not load a file.",null],null],["tf31",0,3,"Why is terraform destroy considered part of the core workflow rather than just a cleanup step?",["It removes all resources Terraform manages in that state","It rewrites the configuration files","It deletes only the resources that have drifted from the configuration","It removes providers from the lock file"],0,"destroy removes everything tracked in the current state, which is useful for short-lived environments. It doesn't touch code or the lock file.","Objective 3f",[null,"destroy never edits .tf files; the configuration stays the same and can be applied again later.","destroy removes every resource in the state, not only drifted ones; handling drift is done with plan or -refresh-only.","destroy does not touch .terraform.lock.hcl; provider selections stay recorded there."],null],["tf32",0,3,"An engineer runs terraform fmt in a directory. What does it change?",["Resource names to match a naming standard","Deprecated arguments to their current replacement arguments","Indentation and alignment to the canonical style","Provider versions to the latest release"],2,"fmt only rewrites layout (spacing, alignment, indentation). It doesn't rename resources or change logic.","Objective 3g",["fmt never renames resources; renaming would change addresses and needs moved blocks, not formatting.","fmt does not rewrite deprecated arguments; it only changes whitespace and layout, so upgrades are manual.",null,"fmt does not touch provider versions; those change only with terraform init -upgrade."],null],["tf33",0,3,"You want terraform plan to skip checking real infrastructure to speed up a quick review. Which option does that?",["-refresh-only","-lock=false","-parallelism=1","-refresh=false"],3,"-refresh=false uses cached state without querying providers. -refresh-only does the opposite: it plans only state updates from reality.","Objective 3d",["-refresh-only is the opposite: it plans only updates to state from real infrastructure, which requires checking reality.","-lock=false only disables state locking; Terraform still refreshes against real infrastructure.","-parallelism=1 limits concurrent operations, which slows a run down and still performs the refresh.",null],null],["tf34",0,3,"During an outage you need to apply a fix to one resource only. Which option limits the run, and how should it be treated?",["-replace, used for all normal changes","-refresh-only, used after every apply","-target, used only for exceptional cases","-lock=false, used by default in every CI pipeline"],2,"-target focuses plan/apply on given addresses and their dependencies; HashiCorp recommends it only for exceptional situations, not routine use.","Objective 3d",["-replace forces recreation of a resource; it does not limit the run's scope and is not meant for all normal changes.","-refresh-only updates state from reality and makes no infrastructure changes, so it cannot apply a fix.",null,"-lock=false removes protection against concurrent writes and should never be a default; it does not limit scope either."],null],["tf35",0,4,"Given: data \"docker_image\" \"nginx\" { name = \"nginx:latest\" }. How do you reference its ID in a resource?",["docker_image.nginx.id","data.docker_image.nginx.id","data.docker_image[\"nginx\"].id","var.docker_image.nginx.id"],1,"Data sources are referenced as data.TYPE.NAME.ATTRIBUTE. Without the data prefix, the address points to a managed resource.","Objective 4a",["Without the data. prefix, docker_image.nginx refers to a managed resource of that type, which is not declared here.",null,"Brackets with a key are for count or for_each instances; this data source has neither, and the address still needs data.TYPE.NAME.","var. refers to input variables, not data sources, so var.docker_image.nginx.id would point to an undeclared variable."],null],["tf36",0,4,"What is the main difference between a resource block and a data block?",["Resources are managed; data blocks only read existing information","Data blocks create objects; resource blocks only read existing information","Data blocks are only allowed inside modules","Resources cannot reference data blocks"],0,"Resource blocks create, update and delete objects. Data blocks read information from providers without managing it.","Objective 4a",[null,"This reverses the roles: resource blocks manage objects and data blocks only read.","Data blocks can be used in any module, including the root module.","Resources commonly reference data sources, for example using a looked-up image ID or AMI."],null],["tf37",0,4,"Given: resource \"local_file\" \"cfg\" { count = 3  filename = \"cfg-${count.index}.txt\"  content = \"x\" }. Which files are created?",["cfg-1.txt, cfg-2.txt, cfg-3.txt","cfg-count.txt three times","cfg-0.txt, cfg-1.txt, cfg-2.txt","cfg-3.txt only"],2,"count.index starts at 0, so three instances get indexes 0, 1 and 2.","Objective 4e",["count.index starts at 0, not 1, so there is no cfg-3.txt.","The ${count.index} template is replaced with the index number, so the literal word count never appears in a file name.",null,"count = 3 creates three instances with indexes 0 to 2, not a single file named after the count."],null],["tf38",0,4,"Given: for_each = toset([\"dev\", \"prod\"]) on a resource. What are each.key and each.value for the first instance?",["\"dev\" and \"dev\"","0 and \"dev\"","\"dev\" and null","\"key\" and \"dev\""],0,"For a set of strings, each.key and each.value are both the element.","Objective 4e",[null,"Numeric indexes like 0 belong to count; with for_each over a set, each.key is the element itself.","each.value is not null for sets; it equals each.key, the set element.","each.key is the element (\"dev\"), not the literal word \"key\"."],null],["tf39",0,4,"A list of five servers is managed with count. Removing the second item recreates or changes several later servers. What change avoids this?",["Add depends_on to every server","Set create_before_destroy on the servers","Use -target so that only one server is applied at a time","Use for_each over a map or set keyed by name"],3,"count addresses instances by index, so removing one shifts the rest. for_each uses stable keys, so only the removed key is affected.","Objective 4e",["depends_on only changes ordering; count still addresses servers by index, so removing one still shifts the rest.","create_before_destroy changes the order of replacement but still replaces the shifted servers.","-target only narrows one run; the shifted indexes remain and later full applies still change those servers.",null],null],["tf40",0,4,"Variable region has default \"us-east-1\". TF_VAR_region=\"eu-west-1\" is set, terraform.tfvars sets \"us-west-2\", and the command includes -var=\"region=ap-south-1\". Which value is used?",["us-east-1 (the default)","ap-south-1 (-var flag)","eu-west-1 (TF_VAR_region)","us-west-2 (terraform.tfvars)"],1,"Command-line -var and -var-file have the highest precedence, then *.auto.tfvars, terraform.tfvars, and environment variables; the default is used only if nothing else sets it.","Objective 4c",["The default is the lowest-precedence source and is used only if nothing else sets the variable.",null,"TF_VAR_ environment variables are low in precedence, below tfvars files and -var options.","terraform.tfvars outranks environment variables but is overridden by -var on the command line."],null],["tf41",0,4,"Given: variable \"ports\" { type = list(number) }. Which value is valid?",["{ http = 80 }","[80, 443]","\"80,443\"","[\"http\", \"https\"]"],1,"list(number) needs an ordered list of numbers. A map, a single string or a list of words cannot convert.","Objective 4d",["A map with a named key is not a list and cannot be converted to list(number).",null,"A single string is not a list; Terraform will not split \"80,443\" into numbers automatically.","The strings \"http\" and \"https\" cannot be converted to numbers, so the list fails the type check."],null],["tf42",0,4,"Which type fits a value like { name = \"web\", port = 8080, public = true } with fixed attribute names of different types?",["map(string)","list(any)","set(string)","object({ name = string, port = number, public = bool })"],3,"An object type has named attributes each with its own type. A map requires all values to be the same type.","Objective 4d",["map(string) needs every value to be a string, but port is a number and public is a bool (Terraform would convert them and lose the types).","list(any) is an ordered list of elements accessed by index, not a set of named attributes.","A set holds unordered unique values of one type and has no named attributes.",null],null],["tf43",0,4,"What does [for s in var.names : upper(s)] return when var.names is [\"a\", \"b\"]?",["[\"A\", \"B\"]","{ a = \"A\", b = \"B\" }","\"AB\"","[\"a\", \"b\", \"A\", \"B\"]"],0,"A for expression in square brackets returns a list with the transformed elements. Curly braces with => would produce a map.","Objective 4e",[null,"A map would need curly braces and a key => value form; square brackets produce a list.","A for expression returns a collection, not a joined string; join() would be needed for \"AB\".","A for expression transforms elements rather than appending new ones, so the originals are not included."],null],["tf44",0,4,"Resource aws_instance.web uses count = 3. How do you output a list of all three IDs?",["aws_instance.web.id","aws_instance.web[3].id","aws_instance.web[*].id","aws_instance[*].web.id"],2,"The splat expression [*] collects an attribute from every instance into a list. Without an index, a counted resource can't be read as one object.","Objective 4e",["A counted resource is a list of instances, so aws_instance.web.id without an index is an error.","Indexes start at 0, so [3] is out of range for three instances, and it would return only one ID.",null,"The splat goes after the resource name (aws_instance.web[*]); aws_instance[*].web is not valid syntax."],null],["tf45",0,4,"A container resource references docker_image.app.image_id in its image argument. What does Terraform infer?",["Nothing, unless you also add an explicit depends_on argument","That both resources must be in one module","That the image must be a data source","An implicit dependency so the image is handled first"],3,"References create implicit dependencies; Terraform orders the graph from them. depends_on is only needed for hidden dependencies.","Objective 4f",["No depends_on is needed; the reference itself creates the dependency.","References work across resources in one module and do not force them into a particular module.","The image can be a managed resource; referencing docker_image.app refers to a resource, not a data source.",null],null],["tf46",0,4,"An application server needs a policy to be attached first, but no argument on the server references the policy. What should you add?",["lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }","count = 1 on the policy","depends_on = [aws_iam_role_policy.app]","A data \"aws_iam_role_policy\" block for the policy"],2,"depends_on declares a dependency Terraform can't see from references. prevent_destroy and count don't affect ordering.","Objective 4f",["prevent_destroy blocks deletion of a resource and has no effect on creation order.","count only controls how many instances exist; it creates no dependency between the policy and the server.",null,"A data block would only read the policy, and the server still would not reference it, so no ordering is created."],null],["tf47",0,4,"Replacing a load balancer certificate causes downtime because the old one is deleted first. Which setting fixes the order?",["lifecycle { ignore_changes = all }","lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }","depends_on = [aws_lb.main] on the certificate resource","lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }"],1,"create_before_destroy makes Terraform create the replacement before destroying the old object. prevent_destroy would block the replacement entirely.","Objective 4f",["ignore_changes = all stops Terraform updating the resource, so the certificate could never be rotated, and it does not change replacement order.",null,"depends_on sets creation order between resources; it does not make the replacement certificate appear before the old one is destroyed.","prevent_destroy would make any plan that destroys the old certificate fail, blocking the replacement."],null],["tf48",0,4,"An autoscaler changes a group's desired_capacity outside Terraform, and every plan tries to reset it. What is the cleanest fix?",["lifecycle { ignore_changes = [desired_capacity] }","Run every terraform apply with -refresh=false from now on","Remove the resource from state","Add depends_on to the autoscaler"],0,"ignore_changes tells Terraform not to plan updates for those attributes after creation. The other options hide or break management.","Objective 4f",[null,"-refresh=false skips reading real values, which hides all drift and can produce wrong plans; it is not a fix for one attribute.","Removing the resource from state means Terraform no longer manages the group at all.","depends_on only affects ordering; it does not stop Terraform planning to reset desired_capacity."],null],["tf49",0,4,"You want terraform plan to fail if var.env is not dev, test or prod. Which block does this?",["A precondition block inside the provider configuration","A check block with an assert","A validation block inside the variable","An output with sensitive = true"],2,"Variable validation blocks reject bad input with your error message. A check block would only warn.","Objective 4g",["Provider blocks do not accept precondition blocks; custom conditions belong in resource lifecycle blocks, outputs and variables.","A check block assertion only produces a warning, so it would not make the plan fail.",null,"sensitive = true on an output only hides its value; it does not validate var.env."],null],["tf50",0,4,"A team wants a continuous health assertion that a website returns HTTP 200, but a failure should warn rather than block the run. Which feature fits?",["A check block","A postcondition","A variable validation","prevent_destroy"],0,"Check block assertions produce warnings and don't stop plan or apply. Postconditions and validations raise errors.","Objective 4g",[null,"A failing postcondition raises an error and stops the run, rather than warning.","Variable validation checks input values, not a website's response, and a failure is an error that stops the plan.","prevent_destroy blocks destroying a resource; it has nothing to do with health checks."],null],["tf51",0,4,"Where do you place a postcondition that verifies an attribute of a resource after it is created?",["Inside the terraform block, under required_providers","Inside the provider block","Inside a locals block","Inside the resource's lifecycle block"],3,"precondition and postcondition blocks go inside a resource's (or data source's) lifecycle block, or in an output for preconditions.","Objective 4g",["The terraform block and required_providers configure versions and providers; custom conditions cannot go there.","Provider blocks configure the provider connection and do not accept postconditions.","locals blocks only define named values and cannot contain precondition or postcondition blocks.",null],null],["tf52",0,4,"A module passes a database password to a provider and the team must keep it out of both plan files and state. Which approach fits Terraform 1.12?",["Mark the variable sensitive = true and rely on CLI redaction","Use an ephemeral variable passed to a write-only argument","Store the password in a local value","Put the password in the output block"],1,"Ephemeral values are never persisted, and write-only arguments accept them without storing them. sensitive only hides display; the value still lands in state.","Objective 4h",["sensitive only hides the value in CLI output; it is still written to the plan file and state.",null,"Local values are ordinary values, so the password would still be stored wherever it is used, including state.","Outputs are saved in state, so putting the password in one makes it more exposed, not less."],null],["tf53",0,4,"What is the recommended way to supply short-lived cloud credentials to Terraform instead of hard-coding them?",["Put the keys in terraform.tfvars in Git","Generate them from Vault or use environment variables","Add them to a README in the repository","Store them as default values in variables.tf with sensitive = true"],1,"Secrets should come from a secrets manager such as Vault (for example dynamic credentials) or the environment, never from committed files.","Objective 4h",["Committing keys in terraform.tfvars puts long-lived secrets into Git history where anyone with repo access can read them.",null,"A README is a public document; secrets in it are exposed to everyone who can read the repo.","sensitive only hides values in output; the default still sits in plain text in variables.tf in version control."],null],["tf54",0,4,"Which command gives an interactive prompt for testing expressions such as cidrsubnet(\"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, 1)?",["terraform show","terraform graph","terraform output -json","terraform console"],3,"terraform console evaluates expressions and functions against the current configuration and state. The result here is 10.0.1.0/24.","Objective 4e",["terraform show prints state or a saved plan; it cannot evaluate an expression you type.","terraform graph outputs a dependency graph in DOT format and does not evaluate functions.","terraform output -json prints existing output values from state and cannot evaluate new expressions.",null],null],["tf55",0,5,"Which module source refers to a local directory?",["source = \"./modules/network\"","source = \"modules/network\"","source = \"local::modules/network\"","source = \"file:modules/network\""],0,"Local paths must begin with ./ or ../; without them Terraform treats the string as a registry address.","Objective 5a",[null,"Without ./ or ../, Terraform treats \"modules/network\" as a registry address, not a local path, and init fails.","local:: is not a valid source prefix; local paths are written as ./ or ../ paths.","file: is not a supported module source scheme; local modules use relative paths starting with ./ or ../."],null],["tf56",0,5,"A module block uses source = \"hashicorp/consul/aws\". Where does Terraform get it?",["From a GitHub repository with that name","From a local folder named hashicorp","From the public Terraform Registry","From the HCP Terraform state"],2,"The NAMESPACE/NAME/PROVIDER format is a registry address on registry.terraform.io.","Objective 5a",["A GitHub source must be written as github.com/... or with git::; a bare NAMESPACE/NAME/PROVIDER is a registry address.","A local folder would need a ./ or ../ prefix; without it the string is not read as a path.",null,"Modules are never loaded from state; HCP Terraform's private registry would need the app.terraform.io hostname in the source."],null],["tf57",0,5,"For which module source can you use the version argument?",["A local path","A plain HTTP URL","A Git repository cloned over SSH","A module from a registry"],3,"The version argument works only for registry sources (public or private). Git sources pin with ?ref= in the URL.","Objective 5d",["Local path modules are read directly from disk and have no versions, so version cannot be used.","Plain HTTP archive sources do not support the version argument.","Git sources pin with ?ref= in the URL; the version argument is not allowed for them.",null],null],["tf58",0,5,"A child module declares variable \"name\". How does the root module give it a value?",["Set TF_VAR_module_name","Declare the same variable in the root module only","Set name as an argument in the module block","Reference module.child.var.name"],2,"Inputs are passed as arguments in the module block. A root variable with the same name isn't passed automatically.","Objective 5b",["TF_VAR_ environment variables only set root module variables, and module_name is not how child inputs are named.","Child modules do not inherit root variables; a root variable of the same name is not passed automatically.",null,"module.child.var.name is not valid syntax; the root can only read a child's outputs, and inputs are set in the module block."],null],["tf59",0,5,"The root module needs the container ID created inside module \"web\". What is required?",["Nothing; reference module.web.docker_container.this.id directly from the root","An output in the child module, read as module.web.OUTPUT_NAME","A data source in the root module","A depends_on in the child module"],1,"Only outputs cross the module boundary. Internal resources of a child can't be referenced directly.","Objective 5b",["Resources inside a child module are private to it and cannot be referenced from the root.",null,"A data source would look up the container by a separate API call, not read it through the module boundary, and is not required.","depends_on only affects ordering and does not expose any value to the root module."],null],["tf60",0,5,"A module should use an aliased provider configuration aws.west. How is it passed?",["providers = { aws = aws.west } in the module block","provider = \"west\" inside the child's resources","region = \"us-west-2\" in the module block","Nothing; aliased provider configurations are inherited by child modules automatically"],0,"The providers map passes specific provider configurations to a child. Only default (unaliased) configurations are inherited automatically.","Objective 5c",[null,"The provider meta-argument takes an unquoted reference, and child resources cannot see the root's aws.west unless it is passed in.","region is not a module block meta-argument; unless the child declares a region variable this is an error, and it does not pass a provider.","Only default (unaliased) provider configurations are inherited automatically; aliased ones must be passed with providers."],null],["tf61",0,5,"A Git-sourced module must stay on tag v1.4.0. Which source is correct?",["git::https://example.com/net.git with version = \"1.4.0\"","https://example.com/net.git#v1.4.0","git::https://example.com/net.git?ref=v1.4.0","git::https://example.com/net.git//v1.4.0"],2,"Git sources select a tag, branch or commit with ?ref=. The version argument doesn't apply to Git, and // selects a subdirectory.","Objective 5d",["version only works with registry sources; Git sources reject it.","Terraform does not select Git tags with a # fragment; a Git ref must be given with ?ref= in a git:: source.",null,"The // syntax selects a subdirectory inside the repository, not a tag."],null],["tf62",0,5,"You raised a registry module's version constraint. Which command downloads the newer module version?",["terraform init -upgrade","terraform validate","terraform fmt","terraform plan -refresh=false"],0,"init -upgrade (or terraform get -update) fetches newer modules allowed by the constraints.","Objective 5d",[null,"validate only checks configuration and never downloads modules.","fmt only reformats files and never downloads modules.","plan uses already installed modules; -refresh=false only skips refreshing state and downloads nothing."],null],["tf63",0,5,"Given: module \"bucket\" { source = \"./modules/bucket\"  for_each = toset([\"logs\", \"data\"])  name = each.key }. How is the logs instance addressed?",["module.bucket[each.key == \"logs\"]","module.logs.bucket","module.bucket[0]","module.bucket[\"logs\"]"],3,"Instances created with for_each are addressed by key in square brackets.","Objective 5c",["each.key is only available inside the module block itself; the address uses the literal key, module.bucket[\"logs\"].","Module instances are not renamed after keys; the module name stays bucket and the key goes in brackets.","Numeric indexes are for count; for_each instances are addressed by their string keys.",null],null],["tf64",0,5,"What is the root module?",["The module in the public registry with the most downloads","The .tf files in the directory where you run Terraform","The first module block in main.tf","A module that holds only provider blocks"],1,"The working directory's configuration is the root module; it can call child modules.","Objective 5b",["Registry popularity has nothing to do with the root module; the root is your working directory's configuration.",null,"A module block calls a child module; the first one is not the root.","The root module can contain any resources, data sources and module calls, not only provider blocks."],null],["tf65",0,6,"No backend is configured. Where does Terraform keep state for the default workspace?",["In .terraform.lock.hcl","In terraform.tfstate in the working directory","In HCP Terraform, which is used automatically when no backend is set","In the provider's cloud account"],1,"The local backend is the default and writes terraform.tfstate in the working directory.","Objective 6a",[".terraform.lock.hcl records provider versions and checksums; it never holds resource state.",null,"HCP Terraform is used only when a cloud block or remote backend is configured; it is never picked automatically.","Terraform keeps its own state; the cloud provider's account holds the real resources, not Terraform's state."],null],["tf66",0,6,"Two engineers run terraform apply at the same time against the same remote state. What prevents corruption?",["The .terraform.lock.hcl dependency lock file","terraform fmt","Provider aliases","State locking by the backend"],3,"Backends that support locking block a second write until the first finishes. The dependency lock file is about provider versions.","Objective 6b",[".terraform.lock.hcl pins provider versions; it does not stop two applies from writing state at once.","terraform fmt only formats files and has no effect on concurrent state writes.","Provider aliases let you use several provider configurations; they have nothing to do with protecting state.",null],null],["tf67",0,6,"A CI job crashed and left the state locked. Nothing else is running. What should you do?",["Run terraform force-unlock with the lock ID","Delete the state file and let the next run rebuild it","Run terraform init -upgrade","Run terraform apply -lock=true"],0,"force-unlock removes a stale lock. Only use it after confirming no run is active; deleting state loses tracking of every resource.","Objective 6b",[null,"Deleting state makes Terraform forget every resource, so the next run would try to create duplicates.","init -upgrade updates providers and modules and does not release a state lock.","-lock=true is already the default; it tries to take the lock and fails again because the stale lock is still there."],null],["tf68",0,6,"Given: terraform { backend \"s3\" { bucket = var.state_bucket } }. What happens at init?",["It works if the variable has a default","Terraform prompts for the bucket later during apply","It fails because backend blocks can't use variables","Terraform silently uses local state"],2,"Backend configuration is read before variables exist, so references aren't allowed. Use -backend-config for dynamic values.","Objective 6c",["Even with a default, variables cannot be referenced in a backend block because backends are set up before variables are evaluated.","Terraform does not defer backend settings to apply; the backend must be fully configured at init.",null,"Terraform does not silently fall back to local state; it reports an error for the invalid backend configuration."],null],["tf69",0,6,"Each environment uses a different state bucket, but the team wants one backend block. What does Terraform support?",["Interpolating terraform.workspace into the backend bucket name","Setting the bucket with TF_VAR_bucket","A for_each on the backend block","Partial configuration with -backend-config at init"],3,"Partial backend configuration leaves out settings and supplies them from a file or key=value with -backend-config.","Objective 6c",["Backend blocks cannot contain expressions such as terraform.workspace, so it cannot be interpolated into the bucket name.","TF_VAR_ variables set input variables, which backend blocks cannot use.","for_each is a meta-argument for resources and modules; backend blocks do not support it.",null],null],["tf70",0,6,"You added a remote backend to a project that already has local state. Which command moves the existing state?",["terraform state push","terraform apply -refresh-only","terraform init -migrate-state","terraform workspace new remote"],2,"init -migrate-state copies existing state into the newly configured backend after confirmation.","Objective 6c",["state push overwrites remote state with a local file by hand; it is a low-level recovery tool, not the normal way to move to a new backend.","A refresh-only apply updates state from real infrastructure and does not move state between backends.",null,"Creating a workspace starts a new, empty state; it does not copy existing local state to the remote backend."],null],["tf71",0,6,"Someone changed a container's settings with the Docker CLI. How do you see the drift without proposing changes to infrastructure?",["terraform validate","terraform plan -refresh-only","terraform fmt -diff","terraform state rm docker_container.web"],1,"A refresh-only plan shows how state would change to match reality without planning infrastructure updates.","Objective 6d",["validate checks configuration only and never looks at the real container.",null,"fmt -diff shows formatting changes to .tf files, not drift in real infrastructure.","state rm makes Terraform forget the container instead of showing what changed, and it changes state."],null],["tf72",0,6,"A refresh-only plan shows a tag was edited manually, and the team decides the manual value is correct. What should they do?",["Apply the refresh-only plan and update the code to match","Run terraform destroy and then apply again to rebuild from the code","Delete the resource from state","Nothing; drift fixes itself on the next plan"],0,"Applying refresh-only records the new value in state, and updating code keeps the next normal plan from reverting it.","Objective 6d",[null,"Destroying and rebuilding causes downtime and throws away the manual value the team decided is correct.","Removing it from state stops Terraform managing the resource instead of recording the correct value.","Drift does not fix itself; the next normal plan would try to revert the tag to the value in code."],null],["tf73",0,6,"Why should state files never be committed to a Git repository?",["Git cannot store JSON files","Terraform deletes and regenerates the state file at the end of every run","They may contain secrets in plain text and cause conflicts","They are too large to commit"],2,"State holds resource attributes including sensitive values, and shared editing through Git has no locking.","Objective 6c",["Git stores JSON files without any problem; the concern is what the state contains and how it is shared.","Terraform updates the state file in place (keeping a backup); it does not delete and regenerate it every run.",null,"State files are usually small; size is not the reason to keep them out of Git."],null],["tf74",0,6,"You run terraform workspace new staging with the local backend. Where is the staging state stored?",["terraform.tfstate.d/staging/terraform.tfstate","terraform.tfstate","staging.tfvars",".terraform/workspaces/staging.tfstate inside the working directory"],0,"Non-default CLI workspaces with the local backend keep state under terraform.tfstate.d/<name>/.","Objective 6a",[null,"terraform.tfstate at the root is used only by the default workspace.","A .tfvars file holds variable values, not state, and workspaces do not create one.","The .terraform directory records the currently selected workspace (in an environment file); local workspace state lives in terraform.tfstate.d."],null],["tf75",0,7,"A container was created by hand. Which approach lets you import it and draft configuration in one plan?",["terraform state push with a JSON file","terraform apply -replace on the container","Write a data block for it, then run terraform apply to adopt it into state","An import block and terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf"],3,"Import blocks are planned like other changes, and -generate-config-out writes starter configuration for the imported resource.","Objective 7a",["state push uploads a full state file by hand and does not import real objects or generate configuration.","-replace destroys and recreates a resource Terraform already manages; it cannot adopt one it does not track.","A data block only reads the container; it never adds it to state as a managed resource.",null],null],["tf76",0,7,"Which command lists every resource address tracked in the current state?",["terraform output -json","terraform state list","terraform providers","terraform graph"],1,"state list prints addresses; state show ADDRESS shows one resource's attributes.","Objective 7b",["output -json prints output values, not the list of resources in state.",null,"terraform providers lists the providers the configuration requires, not resource addresses.","terraform graph prints a DOT-format dependency graph of the configuration, not a list of state addresses."],null],["tf77",0,7,"You renamed resource \"local_file\" \"a\" to \"local_file\" \"config\". How do you avoid Terraform destroying and recreating it?",["Add depends_on = [local_file.a] to the renamed local_file.config","Add moved { from = local_file.a  to = local_file.config }","Run terraform fmt","Run terraform init -upgrade"],1,"A moved block tells Terraform the object's address changed, so the plan shows a move. terraform state mv does the same imperatively.","Objective 7b",["depends_on on local_file.a would reference a resource that no longer exists in the configuration, and it does not tell Terraform the object was renamed.",null,"fmt only reformats files; it does not change how Terraform maps the old address to the new one.","init -upgrade updates providers and modules and does nothing about renamed resources."],null],["tf78",0,7,"Terraform should stop managing a DNS record but leave it in place. Which option does that?",["terraform destroy -target on the record","Delete the resource block and run terraform apply as normal","lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }","A removed block with lifecycle { destroy = false }"],3,"A removed block with destroy = false drops it from state without deleting it. Deleting the block and applying would destroy it.","Objective 7b",["destroy -target deletes the record, which is the opposite of leaving it in place.","Deleting the block without a removed block makes Terraform plan to destroy the record.","prevent_destroy keeps Terraform managing the record and blocks deletion; it does not stop management.",null],null],["tf79",0,7,"A provider fails with an unclear error and you need detailed logs saved to a file. Which settings do this?",["TF_LOG=DEBUG and TF_LOG_PATH=./tf.log","TF_VAR_log=debug","terraform plan -verbose > ./tf.log 2>&1","TF_INPUT=0"],0,"TF_LOG sets the log level and TF_LOG_PATH writes to a file. There is no -verbose flag on plan.","Objective 7c",[null,"TF_VAR_ variables set input variables; TF_VAR_log would only set a variable named log, not logging.","terraform plan has no -verbose flag; redirecting output captures normal output, not debug logs.","TF_INPUT=0 disables interactive prompts and has nothing to do with logging."],null],["tf80",0,7,"You only want detailed logs from the provider plugin, not from Terraform core. Which variable should you set?",["TF_LOG_CORE","TF_CLI_ARGS_plan","TF_LOG_PROVIDER","TF_WORKSPACE"],2,"TF_LOG_PROVIDER sets the provider log level separately; TF_LOG_CORE does the same for core.","Objective 7c",["TF_LOG_CORE sets the log level for Terraform core, the part you want to leave out.","TF_CLI_ARGS_plan adds extra arguments to terraform plan and does not control logging.",null,"TF_WORKSPACE selects the workspace to use and has nothing to do with logging."],null],["tf81",0,7,"Which command shows a human-readable view of the whole current state or a saved plan file?",["terraform validate","terraform fmt","terraform login","terraform show"],3,"terraform show reads state (or a plan file) and prints it; -json gives machine-readable output.","Objective 7b",["validate only checks configuration and prints validation results, not state or plan contents.","fmt reformats configuration files and does not read state.","terraform login stores an HCP Terraform API token and does not display state.",null],null],["tf82",0,8,"A developer wants to run plans from their laptop but have them execute and store state in HCP Terraform. Which workflow is this?",["VCS-driven","API-driven","CLI-driven","Agent-only"],2,"The CLI-driven workflow starts runs from local terraform commands and executes them remotely.","Objective 8a",["VCS-driven runs start from commits and pull requests in a connected repository, not from terraform commands on a laptop.","API-driven runs are started by tools calling the HCP Terraform API, not from local terraform commands.",null,"\"Agent-only\" is not an HCP Terraform workflow; agents are where runs execute, not a way of starting them."],null],["tf83",0,8,"Which block connects a configuration to an HCP Terraform organization and workspace?",["provider \"hcp\" { organization = \"acme\" workspace = \"app\" }","cloud { organization = \"acme\" workspaces { name = \"app\" } }","backend \"local\" { organization = \"acme\" }","module \"cloud\" { source = \"app.terraform.io\" }"],1,"The cloud block in the terraform block sets the organization and workspaces, then terraform init connects to them.","Objective 8a",["There is no hcp provider setting for organization and workspace here; connecting to HCP Terraform is done with the cloud block, not a provider.",null,"The local backend stores state on disk and has no organization setting.","A module block calls a module; it cannot connect the configuration to HCP Terraform."],null],["tf84",0,8,"The same cloud credentials must be available to 30 workspaces. What is the best HCP Terraform feature for this?",["A variable set applied to them or their project","Copying the same variables into each workspace by hand","A terraform.tfvars file in each repo","A shared module output"],0,"Variable sets share variables across many workspaces or projects from one place.","Objective 8d",[null,"Copying variables by hand into 30 workspaces is error-prone and must be repeated whenever the credentials change.","tfvars files are committed with code, which puts credentials in the repository, and they must be maintained in every repo.","Module outputs pass values within one configuration; they cannot share credentials across workspaces."],null],["tf85",0,8,"How do HCP Terraform projects help a platform team?",["They replace workspaces and keep one shared state file for every environment","They store provider binaries for teams","They group workspaces so access and settings apply to the group","They are needed to run terraform fmt"],2,"Projects organize workspaces and let you grant team access at project level. Each workspace still has its own state.","Objective 8c",["Projects do not replace workspaces; each workspace in a project still keeps its own separate state.","Projects do not store provider binaries; providers come from registries, including the private registry.",null,"terraform fmt runs locally and needs no HCP Terraform project."],null],["tf86",0,8,"A security team needs every run blocked if a storage bucket is public. Which HCP Terraform feature enforces this?",["Policy as code with Sentinel or OPA","Run triggers","Cost estimation","The private module and provider registry"],0,"Policies evaluate each plan and can block runs that break rules.","Objective 8b",[null,"Run triggers start a run in one workspace after another workspace applies; they do not block anything.","Cost estimation shows the projected cost of a plan but does not check bucket settings or block runs by itself.","The private registry shares approved modules and providers but does not enforce rules on each run."],null],["tf87",0,8,"The network workspace changes, and the app workspace that reads its outputs must then plan automatically. Which feature connects them?",["Variable sets","Health assessments","Team tokens","Run triggers"],3,"Run triggers queue a run in one workspace after a successful apply in a source workspace.","Objective 8d",["Variable sets share variable values across workspaces; they do not start runs when another workspace changes.","Health assessments check for drift on a schedule; they do not queue runs after another workspace applies.","Team tokens authenticate API access for a team; they do not link workspaces or start runs.",null],null],["tf88",0,8,"Which HCP Terraform feature periodically checks whether real infrastructure still matches a workspace's state?",["terraform fmt -check","Health assessments (drift detection)","The private module registry","Speculative plans on every pull request"],1,"Health assessments run drift detection and continuous validation of checks on a schedule.","Objective 8b",["fmt -check checks file formatting only and never looks at real infrastructure.",null,"The private registry shares modules and providers and does not check for drift.","Speculative plans preview pull request changes; they run on code changes, not on a schedule to detect drift."],null],["tf89",0,8,"What does a VCS-driven workspace do when someone opens a pull request against its branch?",["Applies the change right away to the workspace's infrastructure","Runs a speculative plan and reports it on the pull request","Deletes the workspace state","Locks the repository"],1,"Speculative plans show the impact of a pull request without applying. Apply happens after merge (with approval if required).","Objective 8a",["VCS-driven workspaces do not apply pull requests; applies happen only after changes are merged into the tracked branch.",null,"Opening a pull request never deletes workspace state.","HCP Terraform does not lock the repository; it only reports the plan result back on the pull request."],null],["tf90",0,8,"Which command stores an API token so the CLI can authenticate with HCP Terraform?",["terraform init -token","terraform workspace select","terraform providers lock","terraform login"],3,"terraform login opens a browser to create a token and saves it in the CLI credentials file.","Objective 8a",["terraform init has no -token option; credentials are stored with terraform login or configured in the CLI config file.","workspace select switches to a different workspace and does not authenticate.","providers lock updates the dependency lock file with provider checksums and does not authenticate to HCP Terraform.",null],null]]);
